$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: TYPICAL PROCEDURE Phenol (13 mmol) and KOH (13
mmol) were irradiated in microwave for a specified time between 30 and 90
s depending on the substrate to dissolve KOH. 1-Chloro-4-nitrobenzene (10
mmol) was then added and the mixture was irradiated in modified microwave
oven with stirring for a specified time and power. The end temperature
was recorded after the exposure. The reaction mixture was cooled to room
temperature, ice and 10% NaOH was added to dissolve unreacted phenol. The
reaction mixture was extracted with the solvent and washed several times
with NaOH and then with water. The solvent was distilled off and the
solid obtained was recrystallized from alcohol. The reaction was also
carried out with 53 mmol, (7.9 g) substrate which gave comparative
yields. Table 1. Entry 5, yield 78% M.p. (°C): 54-56
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM microwave irradiation
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Microwave assisted aromatic nucleophilic substitution reaction under
solventless condition
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Rebeiro, Geeta L.; Khadilkar, Bhushan M.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthetic Communications (2003), 33(8), 1405-1410

